From: Jay Leaton <58495321+jayleaton@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 20:25:05 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 63/79] lanes: a forced result lands whole; no hold before its
 last forced row

A window that takes a forced tail whole (a runner's or a tool's result shorter than the
window) empties Stream.force before the commit lands it, so commit's can_hold saw no forced
token and a runner trigger on one of the result's own rows held the stream: the commit broke
there and the rows after the trigger were dropped. DeepSeek-V4.1's P1 run (c69bca6c1) lost
the last 4 of 22 rows of an owned `[exact, computed by the engine: 10*12*2*30 = 7200]` note
this way: its own `10*12*2*30 =` fired an inline cell.

Stream.forced_out counts the rows taken off `force` for the next commit (windows.plan's take,
hold.popForce); commit lands them first and holds only once none is left behind the token.
A trigger on a result's last row still holds as before. test-forced-rows-host on the fake
target: a trigger on each earlier row of a 6-row result never holds and the rows land in order.

A fix applied onto the think-close change.

(cherry picked from commit c8e930375222dee203e9f2c768266cd3507b30f5)
